WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.025
Running local inference begins with loading a compatible GGUF model and sending a prompt to the runtime.

00:00:07.025 --> 00:00:09.727
At first, keep the configuration simple.

00:00:09.727 --> 00:00:16.279
Use a known model, a short prompt, a reasonable output length, and conservative context settings.

00:00:16.279 --> 00:00:25.534
The first goal is to prove that the model loads, the tokenizer works, and the runtime produces output through the path you intend to use.

00:00:25.534 --> 00:00:29.181
After the basic path works, tune operational settings.

00:00:29.181 --> 00:00:33.369
Context length controls how much input the model can consider.

00:00:33.369 --> 00:00:36.071
Thread count can affect CPU performance.

00:00:36.071 --> 00:00:43.434
GPU offload can move supported model computation to the GPU when hardware and runtime configuration allow it.

00:00:43.434 --> 00:00:46.812
Output length controls how much text is generated.

00:00:46.812 --> 00:00:50.459
Sampling settings influence determinism and variation.

00:00:50.459 --> 00:00:55.188
Observe prompt processing speed and token generation speed separately.

00:00:55.188 --> 00:00:58.498
A long prompt may take time before output begins.

00:00:58.498 --> 00:01:02.010
A large or poorly matched model may generate slowly.

00:01:02.010 --> 00:01:06.941
If the system swaps memory or runs out of VRAM, performance may collapse.

00:01:06.941 --> 00:01:15.115
A small benchmark based on your actual workload is more useful than a generic performance claim from a different machine.

00:01:15.115 --> 00:01:21.262
Save working command examples as documented configurations, not hidden one-off experiments.

00:01:21.262 --> 00:01:30.719
Include model name, file path, context length, thread settings, GPU offload choices, sampling settings, runtime version, and hardware notes.

00:01:30.719 --> 00:01:40.040
That documentation lets you reproduce a good setup, compare changes, and recover when an upgrade or model swap causes unexpected behavior.
